WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.068
- I changed my party affiliation

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.503 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I just don't
feel like it's,

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.071
I just couldn't live
with my conscience

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
to be a Democrat anymore
after working on the new book.

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:11.811
I just felt like it's just,
I have questions about even,

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.279 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I'm sticking
with California,

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm going to fight to
save California like you,

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:18.418
but I feel like I'm living

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.120
in Apartheid South
Africa or something,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.756
I just feel like I'm living
in a place that's not moral.

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:26.793
And I feel really bad
and angry about that.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:33.767
We don't want to move, but
the state is pretty terrible.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.302
(gentle music)

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.609 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:45.378 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
is the president of
Environmental Progress

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.280 line:0%
and author of the new book,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
"San Franc-sicko: Why
Progressives Ruin Cities,"

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
Michael Shellenberger, welcome
back to the Rubin Report.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:55.655
- Thanks for having me, Dave.

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.157
Good to see you again.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:01.661
- So last time that I saw you
was about three years ago,

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:06.466
and I think it's fair to say
we both considered ourselves

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:10.003
liberals or Democrats
or something like that,

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.205
that's why I wanted to have
you on, because I thought,

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:15.208
oh, here's someone
sort of on my team,

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
who's frustrated with
some of the stuff

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.079
that I'm frustrated with,

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.848
flash forward three years,

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:24.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
where yet in your little
political journey,

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:27.587 align:start size:97% position:3%
before we fully get into the
book and everything else.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.323 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- As usual, still having
trouble with labels.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:32.258 line:0%
I mean, I wrote an essay called

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.694 line:0%
"Why I Am Not a Progressive,"

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.763
so I'm definitely
not a progressive,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.498 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can't accept
that label anymore.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.634
I changed my party
affiliation in April

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.637
from Democrat to no
party preference,

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is what we call
independents in California.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:51.711
I have two books out, one
last year, one this year,

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:53.580
"Apocalypse Never,"
and "San Fran-sicko."

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.915
I wrote them in part because
I just wanted to articulate

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.750
what I believe,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
based on reading the evidence
and what my views are,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I think labels
are so inadequate.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think some people,
because some progressives

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:08.561
will call me conservative
or a right-wing lunatic,

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:13.566
of course, a lot of people,
I think, call me a moderate.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.803 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Huffington Post,"
quoted me as a moderate.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.906
as usual as you won't
be surprised to hear,

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:21.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, there's some
views I have that I think

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:24.477
would be better categorized
as liberal than conservative

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some views that would
be better categorized

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:27.647
as conservative.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.683
And I think there's some things
that not easy to categorize.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.285
- You know, I should
note for the record

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:37.157 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I actually still
am registered as a
Democrat in Cali,

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.691
but it's a calculated move

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I wanted to be
able to vote in the primary

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that I could vote,
believe it or not for Biden

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.432
in the primary so that
I could stop Bernie.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:50.470
So that sort of tells you how
politics has become so crazy.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.173
And the system is so nuts
that you stay in a party

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:57.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
to vote against the guy you're
trying to stop the most.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.878
- Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, and obviously
the state level,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.383
now we have an open primary
so that you can vote for,

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:06.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can have two
Republicans or two Democrats

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:08.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
or two independents
in the runoff.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.023
But yeah, just, honestly,

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.158
I changed my party affiliation

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I just don't
feel like it's,

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.795
I just couldn't live
with my conscience

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
to be a Democrat anymore
after working on the new book,

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:22.569
I just felt like, it's just,
I have questions about even,

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:23.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I'm sticking
with California,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.406
I'm gonna fight to save
California like you,

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:30.476
but I feel like I'm living in
like Apartheid South Africa

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.011
or something.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.513
I just feel like I'm living
in a place that's not moral.

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:37.450
And I feel really bad
and angry about that.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.020
And so, yeah, we
don't want to move,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the state is pretty
terrible on a lot of levels.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.927
- Yeah, let's sit with
that for a little bit,

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
because as you know, I
fought really, really hard

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.198
for this recall,

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
I basically was campaigning
with Larry Elder.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:57.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
The results of this
thing were a disaster.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:02.275 align:start position:0%
Now Newsome has codified mail-in
ballots, basically forever.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:04.744 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have one party rule,
nothing's working,

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.814
which is so much of what
your new book's about.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.182
Do you have any hope here?

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:14.187 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm just having trouble
seeing the hope.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:15.922
If I could see some
hope, I'd be like,

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.590
I could still stay and fight,

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.158
but I just don't
see that glimmer.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.461 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't see that star
in the distance anymore.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I mean,
it's dark right now.

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.100 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I think the
darkness just also means

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.601
that we just can't see things.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:31.638
And so we should be humble
too, about our own inability

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:34.240
to view what might be out there.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.643 align:start size:88% position:13%
I will say, I think that
the election heavily,

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:38.077
if you believe the polling,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.880 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think it did heavily
turn on the COVID response.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.482 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think most
people in California,

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:44.050
and I don't know, you and
I might not agree on this,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.218
but you I think
most Californians

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.820 align:start size:88% position:13%
has supported the status
quo view of COVID.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:51.824 align:start position:0%
I tweeted something that I
think now is overly pessimistic

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:53.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
about how people voted
for the status quo.

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.795
I don't necessarily
think that's true.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.331
We have polling data that
also shows high levels,

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:03.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
75, 80% support for
basically I think the things,

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.239
the common sense things that
I think you and I will agree

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:07.740
that we need to do about
the open air drug scenes,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
what we euphemistically
call homeless encampments.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.078
So I do think there
is public support

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:13.946
for a lot of the stuff
that we need to change.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:18.184
But yeah, I mean, I think we
have a leadership challenge.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:21.387
I surprised myself and I
did feel like I needed to

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.856 align:start size:97% position:3%
endorse somebody, I ended
up endorsing Kevin Faulconer

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:25.491
in the recall.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.427
In general, I don't
believe in recalls

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:29.796
because I do think you should
give the guy the four years,

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:31.731
but in this case, I thought
the situation on the streets

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:34.400 align:start size:84% position:16%
was so extreme that
it merited the recall.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.668
So I did endorse the recall

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:38.071
and I endorsed Kevin
Faulconer and yeah.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:39.539
And then I defended,
you may have seen,

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:44.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
I defended Larry Elder against
the unconscionable attacks

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:48.214
by the racist Antifa woman,

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.782
or I don't know if she's
Antifa, so don't sue me,

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:53.019
but the racist woman
in the gorilla mask,

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:54.320
throwing an egg at him

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.724 align:start size:97% position:3%
and just the unconscionable
media coverage

00:05:57.724 --> 00:05:59.659
by the "LA Times" in particular,

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:04.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
but really all the state
media, clearly, inappropriate.

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:07.700
I mean, I'm sort of
flabbergasted still

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
by how terrible and biased
the media coverage was.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:14.607
But yeah, with the book and
certainly and a movement

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.309 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we're building, we
have a coalition of groups,

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:18.444 align:start size:88% position:13%
including parents of
kids killed by fentanyl,

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
including parents whose kids
are addicted to fentanyl

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:22.482
and living on the streets and
who want to get their kids

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
off the street, including
recovering addicts,

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:27.987 align:start size:97% position:3%
we are advocating for a pretty
sweeping set of reforms.

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:31.224
But yeah, it was discouraging.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:35.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So when you talk to your
liberal lefty friends,

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:38.631
or just like the average
California type person,

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:40.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
one of the things
that I'm still amazed,

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:42.268
now, I do sense a lot
of people waking up

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:45.071
and I think that the election
and the way they treated Larry

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.240
was a red pill for
a lot of people.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
But still obviously, the
results were basically 70/30,

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.911
so obviously out of the voters

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:55.581
they're down with Newsome
and whatever that means.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:58.217
When you talk to some
of the lefties still,

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:01.187
do they think things
are going well here?

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:02.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
That always seems to be
the confusing part for me,

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:05.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
like who thinks
things are going well?

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.528
- No, I mean, my most
progressive friends

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:10.196
are the guys I play soccer with

00:07:10.196 --> 00:07:12.365
and they don't think it's okay,

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:15.168
they think it's a big problem.

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:16.602
Part of the reason I
wrote "San Fran-sicko"

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:18.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
is because when I would
raise the problems with them,

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:20.907 align:start size:97% position:3%
including the fact that
the vast majority of people

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:22.542 align:start size:84% position:16%
living on the streets
are addicted to

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:25.178 align:start size:88% position:13%
hard drugs or suffering
mental illness,

00:07:25.178 --> 00:07:26.379
they would say to me,

00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:30.416
it sounds like you just
want to arrest everybody.

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:32.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I wrote "San
Fran-sicko" in part to say,

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:34.353
here's what I want to do,

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:35.855
here's what I think the plan is.

00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:38.224
And we don't have to choose
between mass homelessness

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:40.026
and mass incarceration.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:41.661
There is a role for the police.

00:07:41.661 --> 00:07:44.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
There is a role for laws
and law enforcement,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.531
but that's not the
end of the story.

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:46.766
And we don't want to go back to

00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:48.201 align:start size:81% position:19%
"One Flew Over the
Cuckoo's Nest,"

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:50.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's unacceptable
to have these,

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:52.972
100,000, 120,000 people
living on the streets,

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:55.741
using hard drugs, a threat
to themselves and others.

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:57.477
Totally unacceptable.

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.145 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, no, I think that
there's actually high levels

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.847
of dissatisfaction.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:03.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think we are looking
for leadership,

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:06.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether that's coming from
the civil society level

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:07.620
or from the political level.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.755
Somebody has got to step
up and challenge Newsome,

00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:12.458
who obviously is up for
reelection next year.

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:14.627 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So it's kind of funny
you wrote "San Fran-sicko,"

00:08:14.627 --> 00:08:16.162
obviously about San Francisco.

00:08:16.162 --> 00:08:19.532
You happen to be in Venice
right now, just visiting,

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:21.133
I'm in LA at the moment.

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:24.303
So we're only about a half
hour away from each other

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:25.538
and we wanted to
do it in person,

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.573
but my schedule is
a little nuts today,

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:29.075
but what's going on in Venice

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:32.778
because it seems like Venice
has been completely destroyed.

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:36.582
- Yeah, so what we call
homeless encampments

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:39.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
is really a euphemism for
what European researchers call

00:08:39.552 --> 00:08:40.853
open drug scenes.

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:44.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
"San Fran-sicko" really
describes the whole history

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:46.192
of how did we get here,

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:48.528
but basically we're in the
midst of two overlapping

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:51.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
drug epidemics, as you know,
one, the more famous one,

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:52.932 align:start size:84% position:16%
is around opioids
that started with the

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:56.269
over-prescription of
opioids in the early,

00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:58.004
or the late '90s, early 2000s.

00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:00.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
We then curtailed
doctor prescriptions

00:09:00.773 --> 00:09:03.509
in the run up to 2010.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.011 align:start size:84% position:16%
A lot of people
switched to heroin.

00:09:05.011 --> 00:09:07.413
Now a lot of people are
using fentanyl and dying.

00:09:07.413 --> 00:09:11.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
93,000 people died last year,
Dave, from illicit drugs,

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:14.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's scandalous,
it's a moral failing.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.957
That's almost five times more
people than die from homicides

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:19.425
in the United States.

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:22.161 align:start size:97% position:3%
About three times more than
die from car accidents,

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:24.897
up from 17,000 in the year 2000.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.166
So this is a moral crisis.

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:30.303
It's disproportionally
impacting California.

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:33.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
Homelessness increased
in California 31%

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:36.275 align:start size:84% position:16%
over the last 10 years
while declining 18%

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:38.277 align:start size:81% position:19%
in the rest of
the United States.

00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:40.212 align:start size:84% position:16%
So what we're seeing
in Venice Beach,

00:09:40.212 --> 00:09:42.281 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the Tenderloin
Neighborhood of San Francisco,

00:09:42.281 --> 00:09:43.883
in Skid Row in Los Angeles,

00:09:43.883 --> 00:09:47.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also in the
Blade Neighborhood of
Seattle in Portland,

00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:49.422
where I just visited,
I was just in Portland,

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:51.090
but also in Vancouver.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.726
And now we're seeing
very similar situations

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:57.163 align:start size:88% position:13%
in Philadelphia, the
Kensington neighborhood.

00:09:57.163 --> 00:10:00.199 align:start size:97% position:3%
So these are all best referred
to as open drug scenes.

00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:02.134
That's I think the right
way to think about them,

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:04.670
that's the academic
word, but you know,

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.138
there's only one way
to deal with them,

00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:08.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is that you have to
require people to use shelter.

00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:10.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can't let people
camp in public.

00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:13.679
I know that sounds
like a radical idea,
but that's not okay.

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:18.084 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
There is some role for
providing people with housing,

00:10:18.084 --> 00:10:20.152 line:0%
but they should earn
it by making progress

00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:22.655 align:start position:0% line:0%
on their personal plan, towards
recovery from addiction,

00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:27.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
or taking control of their
psychiatric illnesses.

00:10:27.193 --> 00:10:28.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we need a
shelter first policy,

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:30.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
we need a treatment
first policy,

00:10:30.863 --> 00:10:34.367
the housing first policy we
have has completely failed.

00:10:34.367 --> 00:10:36.869
It's basically unethical
because it doesn't actually

00:10:36.869 --> 00:10:39.939
try to deal with the root of
the problem which is addiction.

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:42.708
Everything that we
do in California is
a band-aid solution.

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:44.010
So the solution of Venice Beach

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.111
was just to try to put people

00:10:45.111 --> 00:10:47.113
in some hotel rooms temporarily,

00:10:47.113 --> 00:10:49.782 align:start size:97% position:3%
but everybody just ends right
back out on Venice Beach

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:51.050
or in Tenderloin or Skid Row,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we're not
willing to deal with

00:10:52.551 --> 00:10:55.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
the fundamental problem,
which is an addiction crisis.

00:10:55.021 --> 00:10:57.189
- Right, so that's actually
one of the themes of the book

00:10:57.189 --> 00:10:58.858
that the progressive
and all the cities

00:10:58.858 --> 00:11:00.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
you just mentioned, they're
all progressive cities,

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they sort of
say the right thing.

00:11:02.895 --> 00:11:06.599
Oh, we'll put them in hotels,
they will be off the street.

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:08.100
It sort of sounds right.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then you realize that
now they're just doing drugs

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:12.405
in hotels and no one
suddenly gets a job

00:11:12.405 --> 00:11:13.806
or gets their life in order.

00:11:13.806 --> 00:11:17.009
Is that sort of the
biggest theme to

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:19.645
maybe even a little bit of
your own political evolution

00:11:19.645 --> 00:11:21.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
that progressives
are constantly saying

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:25.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
what sounds right and easy,
oh, we're not going to police,

00:11:25.885 --> 00:11:27.787
we're not going to
worry about drugs,

00:11:27.787 --> 00:11:29.021
we're not gonna do
anything about homes,

00:11:29.021 --> 00:11:30.256
it all sounds good, like,

00:11:30.256 --> 00:11:31.757
oh, we're trying to
be nice to people,

00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:34.694
but the end result is pretty
much everything in your book.

00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:36.128
- Yeah, I mean, this was
one of the big questions,

00:11:36.128 --> 00:11:39.098
which is sort of why do
people who claim to care

00:11:39.098 --> 00:11:42.168 align:start size:97% position:3%
so much about people of color,
the poor, the mentally ill,

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:43.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
the people addicted
to hard drugs,

00:11:43.335 --> 00:11:45.671
why is it that the progressives

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:47.807
have allowed the
situation to worsen

00:11:47.807 --> 00:11:49.909
so terribly in California?

00:11:49.909 --> 00:11:51.711
I debunk all the reasons given,

00:11:51.711 --> 00:11:53.112
people say we don't
spend enough money,

00:11:53.112 --> 00:11:54.447 align:start size:84% position:16%
we spend more
money than anybody,

00:11:54.447 --> 00:11:56.182
and I documented more
money on mental health

00:11:56.182 --> 00:11:58.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
than any other state,
worst outcomes.

00:11:58.484 --> 00:11:59.719
People say it's because
we don't have enough

00:11:59.719 --> 00:12:04.490
permanent supportive
housing, San Francisco,

00:12:05.424 --> 00:12:07.093
it's hard to not
go to the title.

00:12:07.093 --> 00:12:09.361 align:start size:97% position:3%
San Francisco has more
permanent supportive housing

00:12:09.361 --> 00:12:10.629
per capita than any other city,

00:12:10.629 --> 00:12:12.131
and it has the worst outcomes.

00:12:13.532 --> 00:12:16.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
What's going on here is
that this all boils down to

00:12:16.769 --> 00:12:19.338 align:start size:84% position:16%
victim ideology, where
there's this idea

00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:22.208
that we can categorize
some people as victims,

00:12:22.208 --> 00:12:24.243
that nothing should
be asked of victims,

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:25.911
everything should be given,

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:28.848
including the right
to camp in public,

00:12:28.848 --> 00:12:32.284 align:start size:88% position:13%
to use drugs in public,
defecate in public,

00:12:32.284 --> 00:12:35.621 align:start position:0%
engage in even violent
behaviors with no consequences.

00:12:36.622 --> 00:12:39.458
It's specific to the West Coast,

00:12:39.458 --> 00:12:41.360
it's specific to
progressive cities,

00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:44.130
but it is spreading
around the country.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:46.432 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, I mean,
there's been various,

00:12:46.432 --> 00:12:49.034
one of the funniest things
I interviewed progressives,

00:12:49.034 --> 00:12:50.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
I interviewed a lot of
progressives for this book,

00:12:50.803 --> 00:12:54.006
by the way, so they're all
in here and I have my own,

00:12:54.006 --> 00:12:55.608
you know, I worked
for Maxine Waters

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:59.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
to advocate for needle
exchange, in the late '90s.

00:12:59.311 --> 00:13:03.716
I worked for George Soros'
think tanks in the late '90s.

00:13:03.716 --> 00:13:05.317
And I still support-

00:13:05.317 --> 00:13:06.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You have a
progressive pedigree,

00:13:06.852 --> 00:13:08.654
there's no doubt about it, yeah.

00:13:08.654 --> 00:13:11.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So they all talked to
me, everybody talked to me,

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:12.691
and you know, a lot of them,

00:13:12.691 --> 00:13:14.059
it was like, they would
say things to me like,

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:16.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, this really
started with Reagan.

00:13:16.095 --> 00:13:18.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I was like,
really like, how so?

00:13:19.165 --> 00:13:20.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
They would say, Reagan
let everybody out

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:24.170 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the mental institutions,
as governor, in truth,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:25.905
it was actually Kennedy who got

00:13:25.905 --> 00:13:28.307
deinstitutionalization started,

00:13:28.307 --> 00:13:30.576 align:start size:84% position:16%
Reagan didn't do
what he needed to do,

00:13:30.576 --> 00:13:32.211
but it's hard to
blame it on him.

00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:34.013
People claimed that Reagan
cut the budget for housing.

00:13:34.013 --> 00:13:36.248 align:start size:97% position:3%
Really the budget for
housing at the federal level

00:13:36.248 --> 00:13:38.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
stayed the same when
he was president,

00:13:38.117 --> 00:13:40.052
it declined very small amounts.

00:13:40.052 --> 00:13:42.822
Mostly the changes had
nothing to do with him.

00:13:42.822 --> 00:13:44.456
Progressives have been
in charge as you know,

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:46.058
in California for
a really long time.

00:13:46.058 --> 00:13:48.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
So this is not a problem
that they can put on

00:13:48.761 --> 00:13:49.962
anybody else's back.

00:13:49.962 --> 00:13:51.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is really something
they have to own.

00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:53.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in fact, you
know, Gavin Newsome,

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:55.401
who used to identify or
position himself anyway,

00:13:55.401 --> 00:13:57.069
as a moderate Democrat,

00:13:57.069 --> 00:14:00.372
was the person who advocated
the housing first policies

00:14:00.372 --> 00:14:01.874
in 2005.

00:14:01.874 --> 00:14:03.409 align:start size:81% position:19%
By the way, it was
George W. Bush,

00:14:03.409 --> 00:14:07.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
who made that official
federal policy.

00:14:07.079 --> 00:14:10.182 align:start position:0%
So it's really been a bipartisan
policy to some extent,

00:14:10.182 --> 00:14:12.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the idea that you should
just give people housing,

00:14:12.451 --> 00:14:14.920
with no requirements
on behavior change,

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.388
has been a progressive,

00:14:16.388 --> 00:14:19.491 align:start position:0%
it's been at the top of the
progressive agenda for 20 years.

00:14:19.491 --> 00:14:21.827
- Did you talk to any
of the progressives,

00:14:21.827 --> 00:14:25.965
say in San Francisco, here
in LA, like Chesa Boudin

00:14:25.965 --> 00:14:28.300
or George Gascon, these
DA's who have decided

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.103
not to prosecute basic crimes.

00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:32.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or even in New York, they're
doing things like this.

00:14:32.905 --> 00:14:34.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you jump the turnstile,
they won't arrest you.

00:14:34.874 --> 00:14:37.042
So you're incentivizing
people to break the law,

00:14:37.042 --> 00:14:38.544
but you know, if you steal,

00:14:38.544 --> 00:14:42.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's under $899
worth of stuff at a store,

00:14:42.414 --> 00:14:43.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
they basically
won't prosecute you.

00:14:43.949 --> 00:14:47.219 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you could get a PlayStation
5 and you get five games,

00:14:47.219 --> 00:14:50.556
but you take that sixth game
and now you're in trouble.

00:14:50.556 --> 00:14:51.724
I get it, I get it,

00:14:51.724 --> 00:14:53.859 align:start size:84% position:16%
they feel like they're
helping somebody

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:55.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
who might need to
steal a carton of milk

00:14:55.895 --> 00:14:57.129
or something like that,

00:14:57.129 --> 00:14:58.764
not even that that
would make it right.

00:14:58.764 --> 00:15:01.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
But do you ever get a
better explanation on stuff,

00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:02.701
we all see the videos,

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:06.372
and we're like, geez, we
know this is not right.

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:08.841
- Right, what you're describing

00:15:08.841 --> 00:15:12.444
is a selective view of victims.

00:15:12.444 --> 00:15:14.914
So progressives are
really concerned

00:15:14.914 --> 00:15:16.682
with victims of the system

00:15:16.682 --> 00:15:21.687
and they're giving a pass to
victims of other individuals,

00:15:22.288 --> 00:15:23.222
so to speak.

00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:25.257
So to give an example of it.

00:15:25.257 --> 00:15:28.394
Progressives are very
concerned with people

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:31.096
who they feel are being
victimized by police,

00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:33.532 align:start position:0%
than people who are being
victimized by other individuals.

00:15:33.532 --> 00:15:37.136 align:start position:0%
So African-Americans are killed
at a rate 30 times higher

00:15:37.136 --> 00:15:40.472
by other civilians than
they are by the police.

00:15:40.472 --> 00:15:42.741 align:start position:0%
And yet there's a huge movement
around police killings,

00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:44.877
which is advocating some reforms

00:15:44.877 --> 00:15:46.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I think are very
positive and we should do,

00:15:46.712 --> 00:15:48.747
but there's basically
no movement whatsoever

00:15:48.747 --> 00:15:53.752
for reducing homicides among
African-Americans more broadly.

00:15:54.853 --> 00:15:56.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
What about drug
addicts on the street

00:15:56.388 --> 00:15:59.959
who are often stabbed with
machetes by drug dealers,

00:15:59.959 --> 00:16:02.161
if they don't pay
what they owe them,

00:16:02.161 --> 00:16:03.595
that's how it gets enforced.

00:16:05.097 --> 00:16:06.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's just kind of
shrugging of the shoulders

00:16:06.632 --> 00:16:09.368
by progressives, that those
crimes and the drug dealers,

00:16:09.368 --> 00:16:11.503
according to Chesa Boudin,

00:16:11.503 --> 00:16:14.373 align:start position:0%
most of whom are from Honduras,
not all, but a lot of the,

00:16:14.373 --> 00:16:15.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's just in
particular San Francisco,

00:16:15.874 --> 00:16:19.345
but the Hondurans control the
drug trade in San Francisco.

00:16:19.345 --> 00:16:21.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Chesa Boudin has said
that they're victims,

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
the drug dealers are victims
of human trafficking.

00:16:23.716 --> 00:16:25.317
That's not true by the way,

00:16:25.317 --> 00:16:26.685
but because there
are people of color

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:27.953
and they're immigrants,

00:16:27.953 --> 00:16:29.922
they get put in the
category of victims.

00:16:29.922 --> 00:16:31.790
So what I was interested in was

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:36.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
why the selective
categorization of victims.

00:16:36.261 --> 00:16:37.830
And what you come
up with is that,

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.399
is that progressives
are strictly concerned

00:16:40.399 --> 00:16:42.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
with victims of the
system and not concerned

00:16:42.901 --> 00:16:44.470
with people that are
victimized by others.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.372
And so one thing was
interesting to me,

00:16:46.372 --> 00:16:48.273
I'm working on a column called

00:16:48.273 --> 00:16:50.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
the reason they defend
crime is because

00:16:50.342 --> 00:16:52.044
they're against capitalism.

00:16:52.044 --> 00:16:54.346
Which is that when you
find people defending

00:16:55.647 --> 00:16:58.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
the shoplifting, the
complete looting of Walgreens

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:01.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
and drug stores in California,
'cause you can steal,

00:17:01.754 --> 00:17:03.756
as you mentioned,
$950 worth of items

00:17:03.756 --> 00:17:05.824
without any consequence,

00:17:05.824 --> 00:17:08.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people who will defend
that are socialists,

00:17:08.227 --> 00:17:11.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
who say, well, Walgreens
rips off its workers.

00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:14.166
So what you find in the
book in "San Fran-sicko,"

00:17:14.166 --> 00:17:17.369
I document a long history
going back, of course,

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:20.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the '60s of
antagonism, to the police,

00:17:20.973 --> 00:17:23.542 align:start size:97% position:3%
but to the criminal justice
system more broadly.

00:17:23.542 --> 00:17:26.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this goes back really
to the 19th century,

00:17:26.779 --> 00:17:28.680 align:start size:81% position:19%
radical anarchists
and socialists

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:32.184
who viewed crime as
sort of justified

00:17:33.352 --> 00:17:37.289
because it was fighting
back against the man.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.826
And so that's where that
selective victimization

00:17:40.826 --> 00:17:42.027
comes from.

00:17:42.027 --> 00:17:43.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
I felt the need to
write "San Fran-sicko"

00:17:43.629 --> 00:17:47.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I think when
my progressive soccer
buddies read it

00:17:47.266 --> 00:17:48.734
and you kind of lay it out,

00:17:48.734 --> 00:17:50.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
where is this
ideology coming from?

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:54.139
And you realize that, yeah,
it's really that stupid.

00:17:54.139 --> 00:17:55.074
You get to the bottom of it,

00:17:55.074 --> 00:17:57.309
and you're like, wow, that dumb.

00:17:57.309 --> 00:17:59.645
You're like, I thought it maybe
would be more sophisticated,

00:17:59.645 --> 00:18:01.547
but it's just that
stupid at bottom.

00:18:01.547 --> 00:18:03.382
- Let's go there, there.

00:18:03.382 --> 00:18:04.817
- Once you make it explicit,

00:18:04.817 --> 00:18:07.719
I think it's harder for
people to defend it.

00:18:07.719 --> 00:18:09.655
- Does it sort of
impress you almost?

00:18:09.655 --> 00:18:11.690
I mean, I ask almost all my
guests this at this point,

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:14.726 align:start position:0%
it's caused so much destruction,
this radical ideology,

00:18:14.726 --> 00:18:16.261
and destroyed so
many institutions,

00:18:16.261 --> 00:18:18.697
literally destroyed cities,
it's destroying colleges,

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:22.634
it's destroying basically the
fabric of American society.

00:18:22.634 --> 00:18:25.637
Do you somehow admire it
in some weird perverse way?

00:18:25.637 --> 00:18:27.039
I mean, even in the
last couple of days,

00:18:27.039 --> 00:18:28.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're holding this
interview for a few days,

00:18:28.474 --> 00:18:29.875
but in the last couple of days,

00:18:29.875 --> 00:18:32.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
Kyrsten Sinema got
chased into that bathroom

00:18:32.344 --> 00:18:34.413
at Arizona State University.

00:18:34.413 --> 00:18:38.550
And I saw an incredible
amount of progressives

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:41.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
and progressive news
organizations defending her.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:43.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh yeah, you can get
up in their face,

00:18:43.188 --> 00:18:46.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
which by the way is
exactly what Maxine Waters

00:18:46.024 --> 00:18:48.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
has been on the record
saying, get in their face,

00:18:48.327 --> 00:18:51.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you almost
admire their absolute

00:18:51.096 --> 00:18:54.299 align:start size:81% position:19%
relentless pursuit
of destruction.

00:18:56.101 --> 00:18:58.971
- Yeah, I mean there's, and
there's a cunning to it.

00:18:58.971 --> 00:19:01.306
There's a cleverness to the
manipulation of language

00:19:01.306 --> 00:19:02.474
in particular.

00:19:02.474 --> 00:19:04.276 align:start size:88% position:13%
Just take a simple
word like homelessness,

00:19:04.276 --> 00:19:07.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
I traced the genealogy
of this word back,

00:19:07.813 --> 00:19:11.283
it's a propaganda word, it's
always been a propaganda word,

00:19:11.283 --> 00:19:13.752
designed to mix together

00:19:13.752 --> 00:19:16.088 align:start size:84% position:16%
two totally different
groups of people.

00:19:16.088 --> 00:19:18.323 align:start size:84% position:16%
One is the proverbial
mother escaping

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:20.259
an abusive husband
with her two kids,

00:19:20.259 --> 00:19:22.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
who doesn't have anywhere
to go that night,

00:19:22.194 --> 00:19:24.396
mixing her together with people

00:19:24.396 --> 00:19:27.232 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
suffering from schizophrenia,
who were on the streets,

00:19:27.232 --> 00:19:28.500 line:0%
often addicted to hard drugs,

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.569 line:0%
or people that are just
addicted to hard drugs,

00:19:30.569 --> 00:19:32.104 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
mixing those
populations together,

00:19:32.104 --> 00:19:34.573
deliberately confusing
two groups of people

00:19:34.573 --> 00:19:36.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
who require completely
different things.

00:19:36.508 --> 00:19:37.943
And by the way, we
do a pretty good job

00:19:37.943 --> 00:19:40.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
taking care of the mom,
escaping the abusive husband.

00:19:40.812 --> 00:19:42.548
She needs something different

00:19:42.548 --> 00:19:44.283
than someone with schizophrenia,

00:19:44.283 --> 00:19:45.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that person that
has schizophrenia

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
needs something
different than someone

00:19:46.385 --> 00:19:48.086
who's just addicted to heroin.

00:19:48.086 --> 00:19:50.756 align:start position:0%
But the word homelessness
suggests that the only response

00:19:50.756 --> 00:19:52.291
should be a charitable response.

00:19:52.291 --> 00:19:55.761
When sometimes you do need
to involve law enforcement

00:19:55.761 --> 00:20:00.632 align:start size:84% position:16%
to either make sure
the laws are enforced,

00:20:00.632 --> 00:20:01.967
and also to compel treatment,

00:20:01.967 --> 00:20:04.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's not compassionate
to let people die

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:06.505 align:start size:81% position:19%
from drug overdose
on the street.

00:20:07.773 --> 00:20:09.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
Look, if someone wants
to kill themselves

00:20:09.741 --> 00:20:11.276 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the privacy of
their own home, fine,

00:20:11.276 --> 00:20:13.378
but we don't allow people to
kill themselves on the street.

00:20:13.378 --> 00:20:15.981
That's not something
that's consistent with

00:20:15.981 --> 00:20:17.516
a civilized society.

00:20:17.516 --> 00:20:20.419
So yeah, I can admire the
cunning, the manipulation,

00:20:20.419 --> 00:20:22.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there's just something
really sociopathic

00:20:22.321 --> 00:20:23.121
about all of it.

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:25.290
I mean, there's a view.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:26.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
I heard a lot of
people say things like

00:20:26.858 --> 00:20:31.029
our unhoused population and it
just gave me the creeps dude.

00:20:31.029 --> 00:20:34.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was just like, they're
not your population.

00:20:34.132 --> 00:20:36.969
They're not objects
for you to control.

00:20:36.969 --> 00:20:38.837
These are often people
that you're contributing

00:20:38.837 --> 00:20:42.774
in their victimization out
of some hatred of the system.

00:20:42.774 --> 00:20:45.477
So yeah, I it's scary, dude.

00:20:45.477 --> 00:20:49.381
- I remember when I was lefty
and they would always say fam

00:20:49.381 --> 00:20:51.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
like, we're fam and I hated
it because it was like,

00:20:51.717 --> 00:20:54.987
you're not my family, we
may be friends or a group

00:20:54.987 --> 00:20:57.756
and we all kind of have common
cause in some of this stuff.

00:20:57.756 --> 00:20:59.625
But like, you also hate family,

00:20:59.625 --> 00:21:01.493
like they hate the
family structure,

00:21:01.493 --> 00:21:05.797
but we're fam because we have
some like ideological cohesion

00:21:05.797 --> 00:21:07.332
or something like that.

00:21:07.332 --> 00:21:10.102
- There's a lot in that forced,
they call it forced teaming,

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:12.004 align:start size:81% position:19%
that sort of sense
of compliance,

00:21:12.004 --> 00:21:14.940
there's also a lot of menace
there, if you don't conform,

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:17.776 align:start size:97% position:3%
you'll be ostracized and
stigmatized and all the rest.

00:21:17.776 --> 00:21:19.978
- So as you talk
about this stuff

00:21:19.978 --> 00:21:22.314
and as you write this
stuff and go through

00:21:22.314 --> 00:21:25.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
whatever your own
political evolution is,

00:21:26.885 --> 00:21:28.654 align:start size:88% position:13%
you're having a similar
experience to what
happened with me,

00:21:28.654 --> 00:21:30.155
which is I sense you're finding

00:21:30.155 --> 00:21:33.025
that people on the right
generally aren't that bad,

00:21:33.025 --> 00:21:35.027 align:start size:84% position:16%
you've done some stuff
with with Prager,

00:21:35.027 --> 00:21:36.428
scary Dennis Prager.

00:21:36.428 --> 00:21:38.830 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think you've had
conversations with Ben Shapiro

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:41.967
and a couple other people,
how's that been for you?

00:21:41.967 --> 00:21:44.403
And have there been red
lines with those guys

00:21:44.403 --> 00:21:46.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you've said something
that was so outlandish,

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:48.206
so crazily left,

00:21:48.206 --> 00:21:49.908
that they didn't want
to talk to you anymore,

00:21:49.908 --> 00:21:51.410
or didn't want to air something

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:54.713
or wanted to censor something
or silence you, et cetera?

00:21:54.713 --> 00:21:57.115
- No, I mean, that
is shocking, right?

00:21:57.115 --> 00:22:00.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
Which is that if you go
on a conservative TV show,

00:22:00.952 --> 00:22:02.487
your progressive friends,

00:22:02.487 --> 00:22:07.292
it's so upsetting to them that
the friendship is threatened.

00:22:08.493 --> 00:22:09.661
Whereas if you go
on a liberal show,

00:22:09.661 --> 00:22:11.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
nobody on the right
seems to care.

00:22:11.296 --> 00:22:15.701
And so it's clearly,
this is my second book,

00:22:15.701 --> 00:22:17.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm writing a trilogy
and the book's,

00:22:17.502 --> 00:22:18.870
"Apocalypse Never,"
came out last year,

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:21.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
"San Fran-sicko" is
coming out next Tuesday,

00:22:21.406 --> 00:22:22.874
I've got a third
book in the works.

00:22:22.874 --> 00:22:24.509
And these are books really about

00:22:24.509 --> 00:22:27.512
how these threats
to civilization and
the institutions

00:22:27.512 --> 00:22:29.815
that support civilization

00:22:29.815 --> 00:22:33.318
are coming from people in the
grip of a religious fervor.

00:22:33.318 --> 00:22:35.087
This is a fanaticism,

00:22:35.087 --> 00:22:39.858
and what's at stake for, you
know, for when, you know like,

00:22:39.858 --> 00:22:41.126
you kind of like,

00:22:41.126 --> 00:22:43.095
so you go on a show with
somebody you don't like,

00:22:43.095 --> 00:22:45.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
why would that threaten
like a friendship?

00:22:45.831 --> 00:22:47.099
why would that break up,

00:22:47.099 --> 00:22:49.768
why would that be such
a threat to somebody?

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:52.337
It's because they have so
much of their identities

00:22:52.337 --> 00:22:54.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
riding on their
political identity,

00:22:54.473 --> 00:22:56.908
so much of themselves on
their political identity,

00:22:56.908 --> 00:23:00.212
so that people's politics
have become their new gods,

00:23:00.212 --> 00:23:01.246
new religions.

00:23:03.014 --> 00:23:05.117
It's interesting because
when new religions form,

00:23:05.117 --> 00:23:08.286
they are more fanatical
and more apocalyptic,

00:23:08.286 --> 00:23:10.956
than they become over time,
they start more extreme.

00:23:10.956 --> 00:23:13.225
So what's scary is
that this is a new,

00:23:13.225 --> 00:23:15.460
is that this is a new religion

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's asserting its
control over the society.

00:23:19.131 --> 00:23:20.399
We have to push back against it.

00:23:20.399 --> 00:23:22.467
We have to expose
how harmful it is.

00:23:22.467 --> 00:23:24.569
Part of the reason I wanted
to write "San Fran-sicko"

00:23:24.569 --> 00:23:27.639
was it's not just
the universities.

00:23:27.639 --> 00:23:30.942
There's real victims
of these policies,

00:23:30.942 --> 00:23:32.744
93,000 deaths last year.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:34.813 align:start position:0%
I'm not going to say all of
them are due to these policies,

00:23:34.813 --> 00:23:38.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
but we went from 17,000, 20
years ago to 93,000 last year.

00:23:38.717 --> 00:23:40.619
That's a lot of dead bodies.

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:43.321
- What's a non-progressive city

00:23:43.321 --> 00:23:45.390
that's kind of doing
this stuff, right.

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:46.892 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've bounced around the
country, I'm sure you have.

00:23:46.892 --> 00:23:49.561
I went to Salt Lake City, it
was pretty freaking clean,

00:23:49.561 --> 00:23:51.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
everybody was decent, I
didn't see homeless people,

00:23:51.797 --> 00:23:53.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
i didn't see people doing
drugs on the street.

00:23:53.331 --> 00:23:54.800
I was in New York
city last week,

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:56.268
I have to say it was more open

00:23:56.268 --> 00:23:58.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
than I thought it was going
to be, which was nice.

00:23:58.236 --> 00:24:00.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
But there were cops on
basically every single corner

00:24:00.906 --> 00:24:02.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
and almost every
corner was barricaded.

00:24:02.874 --> 00:24:05.911
And there were a ton,
I mean around Midtown,

00:24:05.911 --> 00:24:07.546
a ton of people on drugs

00:24:07.546 --> 00:24:09.581 align:start size:84% position:16%
and people just
laying in the streets.

00:24:09.581 --> 00:24:13.552 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, New York has gotten
worse for sure, though, really,

00:24:13.552 --> 00:24:15.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you say which cities
have done it better

00:24:15.387 --> 00:24:16.855
than progressive
West Coast Cities,

00:24:16.855 --> 00:24:19.624
the answer is all
of them, basically.

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:22.994 align:start position:0%
New York shelters 99, or at
least it did until the pandemic,

00:24:22.994 --> 00:24:25.964
it sheltered 99% of
all its homeless.

00:24:25.964 --> 00:24:30.101 align:start position:0%
We shelter about a third in
California and it's very simple.

00:24:31.503 --> 00:24:33.638
You have to build
enough shelters.

00:24:33.638 --> 00:24:37.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
The progressive homeless
advocates, I put it in quotes,

00:24:37.976 --> 00:24:40.011
have denied funding
for the shelters.

00:24:40.011 --> 00:24:41.179
They've taken money away from

00:24:41.179 --> 00:24:42.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
building sufficient
shelter space

00:24:42.781 --> 00:24:45.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
and put it into this
really expensive,

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:48.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
$700,000 to million dollar
apartment unit housing,

00:24:48.487 --> 00:24:50.755
which is grossly insufficient.

00:24:50.755 --> 00:24:52.491
Miami has done a pretty good job

00:24:52.491 --> 00:24:54.926
in terms of dealing
with this population.

00:24:54.926 --> 00:24:56.628
New York did better.

00:24:56.628 --> 00:24:59.431
I really look at Amsterdam
in the book as my model,

00:24:59.431 --> 00:25:01.399
because it's such
a progressive city,

00:25:01.399 --> 00:25:03.635
it's such a liberal city,
it's hard to be against it.

00:25:03.635 --> 00:25:05.370
Their sex work is
basically legal,

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:08.273
you can smoke marijuana and
go to the Van Gogh exhibit,

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:10.442
it's a very progressive city,

00:25:10.442 --> 00:25:12.143 align:start size:88% position:13%
but yet there aren't big
homeless encampments.

00:25:12.143 --> 00:25:13.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when you talk to them
about how they dealt with it,

00:25:13.778 --> 00:25:17.082
it was always social workers
and police working together.

00:25:17.082 --> 00:25:18.917
So it's not like
there's any mystery

00:25:18.917 --> 00:25:20.785
in terms of how you
solve the problem.

00:25:20.785 --> 00:25:22.854 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's more like why
won't we do it?

00:25:22.854 --> 00:25:24.122
And I think that's
where I tell you,

00:25:24.122 --> 00:25:25.957
it's really about
a victim ideology.

00:25:25.957 --> 00:25:28.927
- Right, so how do
we get the lefties

00:25:28.927 --> 00:25:30.428
out of this a little bit,

00:25:30.428 --> 00:25:32.264
you're talking about sort
of the religious fervor,

00:25:33.431 --> 00:25:35.000
that the ideas are
sort of antithetical

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:36.501
to what is actually working.

00:25:36.501 --> 00:25:38.904
Have you figured out a way to
make any headway with them?

00:25:38.904 --> 00:25:41.172 align:start size:88% position:13%
They generally, at
least from where I'm at,

00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:42.974 align:start size:88% position:13%
seem to double down
when things don't work.

00:25:42.974 --> 00:25:45.076
Oh, it didn't work, not
because of what we did,

00:25:45.076 --> 00:25:46.311
but because of those guys,

00:25:46.311 --> 00:25:48.446 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of like your Reagan
answer from before,

00:25:48.446 --> 00:25:51.650 align:start size:97% position:3%
have you figured out any
tricks in this little period

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:53.351
that you've been doing this?

00:25:53.351 --> 00:25:54.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I don't know
if it's a trick,

00:25:54.319 --> 00:25:55.554
but I do think you have to,

00:25:55.554 --> 00:25:56.988
we have to have
a proper solution

00:25:56.988 --> 00:25:59.724
to the addiction and
psychiatric crisis,

00:25:59.724 --> 00:26:01.359
which is really what is driving,

00:26:01.359 --> 00:26:03.562
what we call homelessness,

00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:05.096
and this is not
to do with crime,

00:26:05.096 --> 00:26:06.898
which is a separate issue we
could, we could talk about it,

00:26:06.898 --> 00:26:08.199
or at least not with homicide,

00:26:08.199 --> 00:26:09.734
but so we've come
up in the book.

00:26:09.734 --> 00:26:12.270
I propose something
called Cal Psych,

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:15.874 align:start size:97% position:3%
which would be basically
taking over from the counties,

00:26:15.874 --> 00:26:17.375
which can't deal
with this problem

00:26:17.375 --> 00:26:19.344
because street addicts are an

00:26:19.344 --> 00:26:21.012
incredibly transient population.

00:26:21.012 --> 00:26:22.080
They move from city to city,

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.115
often to escape law enforcement,

00:26:24.115 --> 00:26:25.984
or just because things go bad.

00:26:25.984 --> 00:26:28.720 align:start size:88% position:13%
We need a single agency
to deal with this.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:32.257
It needs to be a psychiatric
and mental health response,

00:26:32.257 --> 00:26:33.725
an addiction response.

00:26:33.725 --> 00:26:36.428 align:start size:97% position:3%
It also would allow us to
have drug treatment facilities

00:26:36.428 --> 00:26:37.829
in places like Fresno.

00:26:37.829 --> 00:26:40.899 align:start position:0%
If you're addicted and breaking
the law on Venice Beach

00:26:40.899 --> 00:26:44.002 align:start position:0%
and you get arrested and offered
to either to jail or prison

00:26:44.002 --> 00:26:45.503
or to go get drug treatment,

00:26:45.503 --> 00:26:47.305
then there's no reason you
need to be in Venice Beach,

00:26:47.305 --> 00:26:49.240
where real estate values are
really high and expensive,

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:50.175
and it's hard to
build something,

00:26:50.175 --> 00:26:52.077
you can go to Fresno and do it.

00:26:52.077 --> 00:26:54.546
So in my progressive friends
say, what do you want to do?

00:26:54.546 --> 00:26:56.948
I say, I want to do this
thing called Cal Psych.

00:26:58.183 --> 00:26:59.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
If anybody should have
a problem with it,

00:26:59.818 --> 00:27:02.654
it should be conservatives,
it should be libertarians.

00:27:02.654 --> 00:27:05.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I spend the very
last few chapters

00:27:05.357 --> 00:27:07.926 align:start size:88% position:13%
of "San Fran-sicko"
interviewing the leading

00:27:07.926 --> 00:27:10.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
conservative thinkers
on this proposal.

00:27:10.562 --> 00:27:12.764
And they all support
it, so Chris Rufo,

00:27:12.764 --> 00:27:14.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
who's famous for
Critical Race Theory,

00:27:14.633 --> 00:27:17.235 align:start size:97% position:3%
before that he was a
really important researcher

00:27:17.235 --> 00:27:18.970
on homelessness and drugs.

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:20.505
He is on board with this.

00:27:20.505 --> 00:27:23.775
I interview a top scholar
at Manhattan Institute,

00:27:23.775 --> 00:27:25.877 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is an important
center-right think tank,

00:27:25.877 --> 00:27:27.212
they're onboard with this.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.782
Republicans have sort of
privately acknowledged

00:27:30.782 --> 00:27:32.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the state
needed to take over

00:27:32.484 --> 00:27:34.386
psychiatric and addiction care,

00:27:34.386 --> 00:27:35.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it hasn't been
something that has been

00:27:35.754 --> 00:27:37.422
at the center of their agenda.

00:27:37.422 --> 00:27:41.459
So my hope is that some
center-right candidate,

00:27:41.459 --> 00:27:42.927
and by the way,

00:27:42.927 --> 00:27:44.162 align:start size:97% position:3%
it could be a Democrat,
Independent, or Republican,

00:27:44.162 --> 00:27:45.897
it doesn't have
to be Republican,

00:27:45.897 --> 00:27:47.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
will pick up Cal
psych and run with it.

00:27:47.532 --> 00:27:49.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
And we made some headway
with Kevin Faulconer

00:27:49.968 --> 00:27:53.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the recall, he certainly,
I think over time,

00:27:53.938 --> 00:27:57.075
started to understand why you
need to have a single state

00:27:57.075 --> 00:27:59.144
provide this service.

00:27:59.144 --> 00:28:00.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I do think that
would be game changing

00:28:00.812 --> 00:28:04.382 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I think that starts
to look a lot closer to

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:07.152
psychiatry for all,
universal psychiatry,

00:28:07.152 --> 00:28:10.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is something that
progressives should embrace.

00:28:10.188 --> 00:28:13.358
And then the only thing that
comes along with it is that,

00:28:13.358 --> 00:28:14.859 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you're going to have
psychiatry for all,

00:28:14.859 --> 00:28:17.462
you can't just go and do
your hard drugs in public,

00:28:17.462 --> 00:28:19.631
there need to be some
consequences as well.

00:28:20.765 --> 00:28:23.034 align:start size:84% position:16%
When we put that
together with polling,

00:28:23.034 --> 00:28:25.870
we find 70 to 80% support.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:27.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
In fact, we found
stronger support among

00:28:27.572 --> 00:28:29.274
Democrats than Republicans,

00:28:29.274 --> 00:28:31.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
because so many
Republicans felt like

00:28:31.676 --> 00:28:33.645
that was already being
a little bit too soft,

00:28:33.645 --> 00:28:35.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that people should
just go to jail.

