00:37:45.429 --> 00:37:47.832 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think that's still
very much alive here.

00:37:47.832 --> 00:37:51.836
It's why we allow people to
be killed by drug dealers,

00:37:51.836 --> 00:37:52.970
killed by civilians,

00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:56.941
but just everything changes

00:37:56.941 --> 00:37:59.877
when people are perceived as
being victimized by the state.

00:37:59.877 --> 00:38:03.247 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Like literally, if you
bring up black on black crime,

00:38:03.247 --> 00:38:05.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
they'll tell you you're
a racist, I mean,
that's your point.

00:38:05.383 --> 00:38:08.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, yeah, and I mean,
here's the interesting thing.

00:38:10.121 --> 00:38:12.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm interested in hearing
your view of it too.

00:38:12.823 --> 00:38:17.295
I mean, to declare all
black people victims,

00:38:17.295 --> 00:38:19.263
that's totally racist.

00:38:19.263 --> 00:38:21.465 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the idea that all
white people are privileged

00:38:21.465 --> 00:38:22.967
is also racist.

00:38:22.967 --> 00:38:24.235
And when you really kind of
listen to it for a minute,

00:38:24.235 --> 00:38:25.703
you go, so let me
get this straight,

00:38:25.703 --> 00:38:27.505 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're saying that all
white people are privileged

00:38:27.505 --> 00:38:29.240
and all black people are victims

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:30.808
and you think that's not racist,

00:38:30.808 --> 00:38:33.010
that's the most racist thing.

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:35.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
Victimology is actually
bigger than that, of course,

00:38:35.046 --> 00:38:37.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
it encompasses that
kind of racism.

00:38:37.048 --> 00:38:39.884
But it also suggests that
a whole set of other people

00:38:39.884 --> 00:38:42.053
are basically, that
people are powerless,

00:38:42.053 --> 00:38:44.055
that people that you put
in the category of victims

00:38:44.055 --> 00:38:46.590
are powerless, and therefore,

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.259 align:start size:84% position:16%
one of the
consequences of course

00:38:48.259 --> 00:38:50.695
is that we shouldn't do
anything to prevent them

00:38:50.695 --> 00:38:54.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
from being victimized
because doing so

00:38:54.165 --> 00:38:56.901
would only exacerbate
the victimization.

00:38:56.901 --> 00:38:58.703
One of the, sorry, go ahead.

00:38:58.703 --> 00:39:00.471
- Well, you, you know
my feelings on this,

00:39:00.471 --> 00:39:01.639
but I'll tell you this,

00:39:01.639 --> 00:39:03.240 align:start size:88% position:13%
look, I have Larry
Elder coming to my house

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:06.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
for dinner tonight, Larry
Elder, born in South Central,

00:39:06.177 --> 00:39:10.114
dad was a janitor, does not
view himself as a victim,

00:39:10.114 --> 00:39:11.649
and is not a victim.

00:39:11.649 --> 00:39:14.919
And I have one of my best
friends from childhood,

00:39:14.919 --> 00:39:18.556
whose son right now, who's
white, which doesn't matter,

00:39:18.556 --> 00:39:20.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
other than for the
purposes of this story,

00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:22.226
his son is hooked on
fentanyl right now.

00:39:22.226 --> 00:39:25.129
And and it's like,
he grew up privileged

00:39:25.129 --> 00:39:26.430
in that his dad had a job.

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:28.566
So it's like, none of
this makes any sense.

00:39:28.566 --> 00:39:30.067
- I'll give you, I'll
tell you something.

00:39:30.067 --> 00:39:33.237
I've been obsessed with this,

00:39:33.237 --> 00:39:35.906
with one of the fathers of
cognitive behavioral therapy

00:39:35.906 --> 00:39:37.575
named Victor Frankl.

00:39:37.575 --> 00:39:39.009
Who's famous because
he wrote a book called

00:39:39.009 --> 00:39:40.478
"Man's Search for Meaning,"

00:39:40.478 --> 00:39:43.147 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he's one of the original
humanistic psychologists

00:39:43.147 --> 00:39:45.182
of the 1960s.

00:39:45.182 --> 00:39:46.617
His whole book, "Man's
Search For Meaning,"

00:39:46.617 --> 00:39:49.487
is about how he survived
the concentration camps

00:39:49.487 --> 00:39:50.988
under the Nazis.

00:39:50.988 --> 00:39:55.259
And he did so by changing his
mentality and getting a goal

00:39:55.259 --> 00:39:58.562
and his goal was, survive
the concentration camps,

00:40:00.297 --> 00:40:02.066
find my wife and parents,

00:40:02.066 --> 00:40:04.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
and write a book
about the experience.

00:40:04.201 --> 00:40:07.104 align:start position:0%
And so his whole thing was
mentality, mentality, mentality,

00:40:07.104 --> 00:40:08.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
forget about how bad
the situation is,

00:40:08.906 --> 00:40:11.008
mentality is everything.

00:40:11.008 --> 00:40:13.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he was loved by
progressives in the '60s.

00:40:13.878 --> 00:40:15.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
Viktor Frankl's book,
"Man's Search for Meaning."

00:40:15.513 --> 00:40:17.081
He sold like 10 million copies.

00:40:18.516 --> 00:40:20.117
During the pandemic, when
I was in some dark moments,

00:40:20.117 --> 00:40:22.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd watch like these five
minute YouTube videos

00:40:22.153 --> 00:40:23.754
of Victor Frankel talking,

00:40:23.754 --> 00:40:26.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
and suddenly I felt
enthusiastic about life.

00:40:26.824 --> 00:40:29.093
And if you take Victor Frankel's

00:40:29.093 --> 00:40:31.862
core message of empowerment,

00:40:31.862 --> 00:40:34.031
which I think is the
Larry Elder message,

00:40:34.031 --> 00:40:35.933
that you are describing as well,

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:37.835 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is that you
can change your life,

00:40:37.835 --> 00:40:40.004
you can take control and be
responsible for your life,

00:40:40.004 --> 00:40:43.841
but somehow progressive said,
that's fine for self-help,

00:40:43.841 --> 00:40:46.977
but in politics, it's
blaming the victim,

00:40:46.977 --> 00:40:49.847
to suggest that people have
control over their lives,

00:40:49.847 --> 00:40:52.383
to suggest that people
responsibility and agency

00:40:52.383 --> 00:40:54.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
and empowerment was viewed
as blaming the victim.

00:40:54.885 --> 00:40:58.789
And boy, what a disservice
and what a destructive view,

00:40:58.789 --> 00:41:01.091 align:start size:97% position:3%
this idea that you should
categorize people as victims

00:41:01.091 --> 00:41:03.194
and suggest they're powerless.

00:41:03.194 --> 00:41:05.763 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well that's why I always
say, it's anti-human actually,

00:41:05.763 --> 00:41:08.599
because the one thing that
separates us from the animals

00:41:08.599 --> 00:41:11.435
is our ability to logic
and reason and dream

00:41:11.435 --> 00:41:15.573
and pursue and have passion
for something and build,

00:41:15.573 --> 00:41:17.475
that was a lot of things that
I combined into one thing,

00:41:17.475 --> 00:41:19.643 align:start size:88% position:13%
but like, that's the
thing, that's the thing.

00:41:19.643 --> 00:41:21.745
And they're saying,
no, it's not yours,

00:41:21.745 --> 00:41:24.248 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's ours to
somehow give to you.

00:41:24.248 --> 00:41:27.818
- And that seems to me,
in terms of fighting back

00:41:27.818 --> 00:41:30.821
against the radical left
and the dehumanization

00:41:30.821 --> 00:41:32.723
that you're describing
and the disempowerment,

00:41:32.723 --> 00:41:35.526
that seems to me, to be at
the soft underbelly as well.

00:41:35.526 --> 00:41:37.561
We, I think have
a natural instinct

00:41:37.561 --> 00:41:40.397
to not want to perceive
ourselves as victims.

00:41:40.397 --> 00:41:41.899
In fact, in the book,

00:41:41.899 --> 00:41:45.336 line:0%
I describe a fair amount of
homeless guys will confess

00:41:45.336 --> 00:41:47.404 line:0%
that they were just
playing the victim,

00:41:47.404 --> 00:41:49.073 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
that they don't
actually perceive them,

00:41:49.073 --> 00:41:50.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
homeless guys living on
the streets don't actually

00:41:50.641 --> 00:41:53.143
perceive themselves as victims.

00:41:53.143 --> 00:41:54.545
And I think in part,
because it's like,

00:41:54.545 --> 00:41:56.080
that's a terrible way to live.

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:59.717
It's a depressive way to live
and it's hard to sustain it.

00:42:00.885 --> 00:42:02.386 align:start size:81% position:19%
They might pretend
that for a bit.

00:42:02.386 --> 00:42:03.654
But I think that really,

00:42:03.654 --> 00:42:05.289
you're onto something
really powerful here,

00:42:05.289 --> 00:42:07.591
which is, and I think that
all of your work, honestly,

00:42:07.591 --> 00:42:11.896
Dave, has been really
creative and productive

00:42:11.896 --> 00:42:13.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you're talking
about this question of

00:42:13.797 --> 00:42:15.766
psychological health,

00:42:15.766 --> 00:42:17.601
that the extension of
psychological health

00:42:17.601 --> 00:42:22.072
and one's own life in politics
should be one of empowerment.

00:42:22.072 --> 00:42:24.141
I don't think that means that,

00:42:24.141 --> 00:42:26.210
I think when it comes to
things like healthcare,

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:29.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we still may need a
universal psychiatric program,

00:42:29.747 --> 00:42:32.149 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it has to be paired
with this idea that

00:42:32.149 --> 00:42:35.819
people are capable of taking
responsibility for their lives,

00:42:35.819 --> 00:42:38.722
even people, frankly, with
disabilities and addictions

00:42:38.722 --> 00:42:40.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
and in all sorts of
terrible circumstances,

00:42:40.391 --> 00:42:43.060
all the more reason that
they should be empowered

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:45.262 align:start position:0%
to take power and
responsibility for their lives.

00:42:45.262 --> 00:42:46.797
- And of course,
that's the irony

00:42:46.797 --> 00:42:50.134
because as I've become, say
more libertarian in general,

00:42:50.134 --> 00:42:51.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
and people say, well, you
don't care about these people

00:42:51.969 --> 00:42:53.237
or something like that.

00:42:53.237 --> 00:42:55.406
Now at first off, it's
not an honest statement,

00:42:55.406 --> 00:42:56.674
because it's like,

00:42:56.674 --> 00:42:58.175 align:start size:88% position:13%
they're usually doing
nothing for them either,

00:42:58.175 --> 00:42:59.910 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're just screaming about
it, they're pretending,

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:02.513
or they want the state to do
it, but they're pretending.

00:43:02.513 --> 00:43:04.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think even bigger
than that is sort of like,

00:43:04.915 --> 00:43:07.251
well, if any of
the stuff worked,

00:43:07.251 --> 00:43:09.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you gave me
policies that worked,

00:43:09.019 --> 00:43:10.588
some of the things you
were describing here,

00:43:10.588 --> 00:43:13.190
then it's like, oh, we should
fund those things properly.

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:15.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
But if you consistently show
me things that don't work

00:43:15.826 --> 00:43:18.596
and keep people trapped
in this cycle of poverty

00:43:18.596 --> 00:43:20.464 align:start size:84% position:16%
and everything else,
well then yeah,

00:43:20.464 --> 00:43:21.765 align:start size:81% position:19%
I don't need my
money going to it.

00:43:21.765 --> 00:43:23.000
And it's not even about money,

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:24.168
I just don't need,

00:43:24.168 --> 00:43:26.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's not how society
should operate.

00:43:27.137 --> 00:43:28.405
- That's right,

00:43:28.405 --> 00:43:31.375
and if any advice comes
out of "San Fran-sicko"

00:43:31.375 --> 00:43:33.377
for libertarians
and for the right,

00:43:33.377 --> 00:43:35.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's just that we should
lead with what we want to do,

00:43:35.846 --> 00:43:37.314 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is, I think that's
why I sort of say,

00:43:37.314 --> 00:43:39.383
I want the modified, I
want to modify Dutch,

00:43:39.383 --> 00:43:41.418
I just want to do
what the Dutch do.

00:43:41.418 --> 00:43:43.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, why do I want
to do what the Dutch do?

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:45.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's some ideological
predisposition,

00:43:45.389 --> 00:43:48.392
it's because it works so darn
well, there's nobody on the,

00:43:48.392 --> 00:43:49.627 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's hardly
anybody on the street,

00:43:49.627 --> 00:43:51.895
very few people on the street,

00:43:51.895 --> 00:43:55.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
marijuana is basically
decriminalized,

00:43:55.332 --> 00:43:58.869
but they have the most humane
treatment of the mentally ill,

00:43:58.869 --> 00:44:01.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
the most humane
treatment of addicts.

00:44:01.005 --> 00:44:03.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's exactly what you said,
but it's got a balance.

00:44:03.674 --> 00:44:05.676
We've got to restore
some balance here.

00:44:05.676 --> 00:44:06.844
And I think that's
the kind of thing

00:44:06.844 --> 00:44:08.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
that my progressive
soccer buddies

00:44:08.979 --> 00:44:11.715
and my hairdresser and the
folks that voted for Bernie,

00:44:11.715 --> 00:44:13.350 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that's what the
vast majority of them want.

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:14.818
They just want to see
some balance restored,

00:44:14.818 --> 00:44:16.286
that if we're going
to give something,

00:44:16.286 --> 00:44:17.755 align:start size:84% position:16%
we should get
something in return.

00:44:17.755 --> 00:44:19.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, you know, it's
interesting on the sort of

00:44:19.623 --> 00:44:21.558 align:start size:81% position:19%
be careful what
you ask for thing,

00:44:21.558 --> 00:44:23.861
I'm completely for
legalizing medical

00:44:23.861 --> 00:44:25.329
and well medical, certainly,

00:44:25.329 --> 00:44:27.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
and recreational marijuana
and some psychedelics

00:44:27.698 --> 00:44:29.233
and things like that.

00:44:29.233 --> 00:44:30.734
But I will tell you when I
was in New York last week,

00:44:30.734 --> 00:44:34.304 align:start size:97% position:3%
a lot of the street corners
that used to smell like urine,

00:44:34.304 --> 00:44:35.539
they smell like weed.

00:44:35.539 --> 00:44:38.809
I'm actually not sure if
that's good, you know,

00:44:38.809 --> 00:44:41.945
it just is what
it is, I suppose.

00:44:41.945 --> 00:44:44.081
- No, I appreciate
you saying that,

00:44:44.081 --> 00:44:46.316
I have some real concerns about

00:44:46.316 --> 00:44:51.321
what we might call the IDW's
excitement about psychedelics.

00:44:52.523 --> 00:44:54.058
It's showing up on
my Instagram page

00:44:54.058 --> 00:44:55.392
that I can take psychedelics

00:44:55.392 --> 00:44:57.127
and there's no downside to them.

00:44:58.262 --> 00:45:00.130
I've become much more
sensitive to that.

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:03.600
I've always been an advocate
of decriminalizing marijuana.

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:08.338 align:start size:97% position:3%
My view is that we have
amazing drugs available to us,

00:45:08.338 --> 00:45:09.907
more than ever in history,

00:45:09.907 --> 00:45:12.943
and they're very powerful
and they're different,

00:45:14.445 --> 00:45:16.980
we have to have more
complex view of drugs,

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.615
drugs are different
from one another,

00:45:18.615 --> 00:45:20.718
nobody overdoses from marijuana,

00:45:20.718 --> 00:45:22.486 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a little bit of
fentanyl can kill you.

00:45:22.486 --> 00:45:23.587
So these are two
different drugs.

00:45:23.587 --> 00:45:25.089
But you can abuse marijuana

00:45:25.089 --> 00:45:27.658
and marijuana can have
really harmful impacts.

00:45:27.658 --> 00:45:29.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so this is again where
you just have to have

00:45:29.359 --> 00:45:30.728
a system of care.

00:45:30.728 --> 00:45:34.898
You know, we know
that ecstasy, MDMA,

00:45:34.898 --> 00:45:36.834 align:start size:88% position:13%
works well with victims
of extreme trauma

00:45:36.834 --> 00:45:39.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are having a hard time
overcoming their PTSD.

00:45:39.703 --> 00:45:42.306 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's some evidence that
some of the other psychedelics

00:45:42.306 --> 00:45:45.008
have some benefit
as antidepressants.

00:45:45.008 --> 00:45:47.411
They need to be done under
supervised circumstances.

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:51.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, this is wild
west out here, and again,

00:45:51.715 --> 00:45:54.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think this has been a
traditional sensitive issue,

00:45:54.718 --> 00:45:56.720
both for right libertarians,

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.123 align:start size:88% position:13%
but also for left
libertarians like ACLU.

00:45:59.123 --> 00:46:02.326
But I kind of go, I don't
know what the problem is

00:46:02.326 --> 00:46:05.362 align:start size:97% position:3%
of having psychiatry
involved in helping to guide

00:46:05.362 --> 00:46:07.965
people's use of those drugs,

00:46:07.965 --> 00:46:10.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
because maybe they'll have
some benefit for people

00:46:10.768 --> 00:46:12.202
or maybe they won't,

00:46:12.202 --> 00:46:13.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe they would be
better off taking an SSRI,

00:46:13.871 --> 00:46:16.106
maybe medical marijuana
is better for some people

00:46:16.106 --> 00:46:17.040
than an SSRI,

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:18.308
but you do need some kind of

00:46:18.308 --> 00:46:20.177
professional care
involved in that.

00:46:20.177 --> 00:46:23.247
Or I think you just end
up with 120,000 people

00:46:23.247 --> 00:46:24.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
on the streets
addicted to hard drugs.

00:46:24.982 --> 00:46:27.151
- The best lines, since
you mentioned the IDW,

00:46:27.151 --> 00:46:29.019
the best line that I
heard on psychedelics,

00:46:29.019 --> 00:46:30.721
was when I was on
tour with Jordan,

00:46:30.721 --> 00:46:31.955 align:start size:81% position:19%
people would often
ask in the Q&amp;A,

00:46:31.955 --> 00:46:33.524
what do you think
about psychedelics?

00:46:33.524 --> 00:46:35.692
What do you think about magic
mushrooms, blah, blah, blah.

00:46:35.692 --> 00:46:37.494
And his answer was
always the same,

00:46:37.494 --> 00:46:39.863
be wary of unearned wisdom.

00:46:40.964 --> 00:46:43.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I always thought
that was like,

00:46:43.100 --> 00:46:45.969 align:start size:84% position:16%
you kind of said it
all pretty succinctly.

00:46:45.969 --> 00:46:47.104
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:46:47.104 --> 00:46:48.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think we
under-emphasize things

00:46:48.872 --> 00:46:50.374
that are probably more boring,

00:46:50.374 --> 00:46:51.875
but at least for me personally,

00:46:51.875 --> 00:46:53.677 align:start size:97% position:3%
in my own mental health have
been much more important,

00:46:53.677 --> 00:46:56.780
like exercise, it's so boring,

00:46:56.780 --> 00:46:58.348
and everyone gets sick
of me talking about,

00:46:58.348 --> 00:47:00.217
but I'm kind of like
if I don't exercise

00:47:00.217 --> 00:47:01.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
like a hard exercise
every other day,

00:47:01.752 --> 00:47:04.154
then I'm just not
right mentally.

00:47:04.154 --> 00:47:05.956
And I think there's just a
lot of people where I'm like,

00:47:05.956 --> 00:47:09.860 align:start position:0%
I see people looking for a
kind of a medicine or something

00:47:09.860 --> 00:47:11.695
to take rather than
doing other things

00:47:11.695 --> 00:47:13.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are much better
for self care.

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:16.867 align:start size:97% position:3%
- that's actually where I
wanted to end this with you.

00:47:16.867 --> 00:47:18.669
That as you've been
on this journey,

00:47:18.669 --> 00:47:21.071
I wonder, just your
life in general,

00:47:21.071 --> 00:47:24.808 align:start position:0%
whether it's your relationships,
physical body, mental,

00:47:24.808 --> 00:47:29.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
spiritual, I can say my
life is significantly better

00:47:29.313 --> 00:47:31.281
on all fronts.

00:47:31.281 --> 00:47:34.852
Confronting the ideologies
that I had to break through

00:47:34.852 --> 00:47:38.155
and let go of some things and
traverse that new territory

00:47:38.155 --> 00:47:39.790 align:start size:88% position:13%
and find out that people
I thought were scary,

00:47:39.790 --> 00:47:41.024
aren't that scary,

00:47:41.024 --> 00:47:42.226
and some of the things
that I believe weren't,

00:47:42.226 --> 00:47:44.161 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then that translated
into actually taking

00:47:44.161 --> 00:47:46.897 align:start size:84% position:16%
better care of
myself, physically,
mentally, spiritually,

00:47:46.897 --> 00:47:48.131
and everything else.

00:47:48.131 --> 00:47:50.234
I sense you're kind
of in a similar spot.

00:47:51.335 --> 00:47:52.769
- I am, thank you for asking.

00:47:52.769 --> 00:47:55.072 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I really appreciate
how you shared

00:47:55.072 --> 00:47:57.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
your own personal journey with
your videos over the years.

00:47:57.674 --> 00:48:01.345
I've found it really
inspiring and brave.

00:48:01.345 --> 00:48:02.913 align:start size:81% position:19%
'Cause I know it's
not easy to do,

00:48:02.913 --> 00:48:04.147
to share some of these things,

00:48:04.147 --> 00:48:05.949
it's certainly not easy for me.

00:48:05.949 --> 00:48:07.384 align:start size:81% position:19%
Yeah, I mean, just
like you said,

00:48:07.384 --> 00:48:11.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
I basically couldn't
finish "Apocalypse Never"

00:48:11.154 --> 00:48:12.656
until I came back to my faith,

00:48:12.656 --> 00:48:14.925
until I felt like,

00:48:14.925 --> 00:48:17.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
until I stopped being
embarrassed about my faith

00:48:17.361 --> 00:48:20.130 align:start size:84% position:16%
and believing actually
that it was okay

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:21.798
for my psychological
health to have faith.

00:48:21.798 --> 00:48:23.800 align:start size:84% position:16%
In fact, that was
quite important to it.

00:48:23.800 --> 00:48:28.071
I also stopped drinking three
years ago, almost exactly.

00:48:28.071 --> 00:48:29.439
And it was one of
the best decisions

00:48:29.439 --> 00:48:31.742
I ever made for my life.

00:48:33.277 --> 00:48:37.915
I think about how to talk
about it and in the end,

00:48:37.915 --> 00:48:40.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
I decided not to write about
that in "San Fran-sicko,"

00:48:40.317 --> 00:48:41.852
which is nice that
you ask about it,

00:48:41.852 --> 00:48:44.187
because I felt like I didn't
want to compare my situation

00:48:44.187 --> 00:48:45.722
to people on the street

00:48:45.722 --> 00:48:47.791
'cause that's a really
different situation,

00:48:47.791 --> 00:48:48.725
but it did make my life better,

00:48:48.725 --> 00:48:50.327
and it was what I needed to do

00:48:50.327 --> 00:48:52.629
because I wasn't able to
get control of my drinking

00:48:52.629 --> 00:48:54.231
in a moderate way.

00:48:54.231 --> 00:48:58.735
And for sure, all the things
that you hear really matter,

00:49:00.103 --> 00:49:04.174
family, faith, sobriety,

00:49:04.174 --> 00:49:05.909
and then I would
just add exercise,

00:49:06.877 --> 00:49:08.779
have been really
important for me

00:49:08.779 --> 00:49:11.014
in dealing with tough times.

00:49:11.014 --> 00:49:12.683
- Crazy right-wing stuff, huh.

